british supermarkets including iceland  and tesco are threatening to stop using  brazilian agricultural products  if the country passes a law expanding  property rights for squatters on public  land  environmental campaigners warned that  the law will encourage deforestation  as it would reward land grabbers in the  amazon rainforest who occupy properties  illegally well joining me now is  iceland's managing director richard  walker  richard thank you so much for talking to  us this afternoon why did you sign this  letter  well i think the the scale of the crisis  the urgency of the crisis cannot be  overstated uh last year alone there was  a size  of virgin rainforest burnt down in  brazil six times  the size of london and this legislative  package  that's going through the brazilian  parliament at the moment  will open up tens of millions more  hectares  of amazon rainforest uh to be uh burnt  and chopped down and also very  importantly it'll open up  indigenous lands for development and  exploitation as well  people who defend this bill say that  actually it's  going to so it will allow land that has  been illegally occupied  from 2014 to be bought so they argue  that people who um small-scale farmers  for example  can clarify title deeds to land meaning  that they can buy  the land that they're on if they've been  illegally um  squatting on it if that's what you want  to call it um title deeds are such an  issue  and small-scale farming is so difficult  do you not think that we should be  getting involved in something like this  from over here well i think this is  humanity's problem because  um at some point in the not too distant  future within a matter of years  the amazon rainforest will turn from a  net  carbon sink to a net carbon emitter  which will have catastrophic  uh consequences for all of humanity and  therefore  the best thing we can do right now is to  stop chopping it down  um but we also of course need to need to  protect  indigenous rights and local land holders  and we work alongside greenpeace quite  closely  and have taken um soundings from wwf as  well  and what what we're told um is that this  is very much about the bolsonaro  government and his cronies uh  taking a land grab to the amazon and  shifting the baseline  away from um those people who are the  indigenous rights holders to the land so  what are the indigenous groups saying  about this  well um i i think there's been  well-documented reports of uh abuses and  land grabs  in um in the amazon um and of course  as a british supermarket sector it's  it's incumbent on  us to take responsibility for our own  supply chains we are having direct  impacts  on these rights abuses because we sell  chicken and pork that has been fed on  soy  which comes from the amazon and uh we  cannot be sure no supermarket can  um that that soy is deforestation free  and therefore  we really are reaching an end point now  um  where we have to say enough is enough  and uh if they won't listen to us like  they did a year ago and they and they  stopped the legislation going through  parliament then  um because money talks but if they won't  listen to us again then  i think we do have to consider um an  option of a moratorium  on brazilian soy what you're talking  about is pretty dire  the situation there don't you think  you've already reached that point  where the supermarkets should say enough  is enough we're not going to deal with  you  potentially yes um but i think you know  a boycott if you like is is not the way  to go a moratorium  um potentially is but it's not a light  switch we can flick  these are proper uh systemic problems  that require systemic  um solutions and therefore we need to  work with the uh commodity traders like  cargill as well  and work on segregation of supply with  them  and so that potentially we can look at  other sourcing options from south  america or america  or indeed europe at the moment it's  impossible to segregate because  um the traders aren't choosing to  to do so um so yes i think drastic times  do call for drastic measures and we do  need to  um look very closely at the situation  and take  what do everything we can to try and  stop this this environmental tragedy but  also  um this local tragedy as well what do  you think  jai bolsonaro is going to make it this  letter if he even takes any note of it  of  notice of it at all i think a year ago  you know it did surprise me because  letters are easy to sign right but  action is  is very different but actually money  talks and  um the biggest market for uh brazilian  soy is europe and and china increasingly  um but if if a lot of the end buyers of  this product are saying we don't we  don't want to be complicit  in uh amazon destruction anymore um then  they have to sit up and and take notice  uh like they did a year ago and  hopefully like they will next week as  well  why are they trying it again then  i don't know um and i'm certainly no  domestic uh  expert on brazilian um policies but it  is odd  and certainly completely at odds with  the rhetoric  um of the bolsonaro regime  last week when at a meeting of world  leaders uh virtually with president  um biden they pledged to halt  deforestation and  do lots of things to improve um the  local environment in the amazon  this will have the exact opposite um  effect  uh tens of millions of hectares of  tropical rainforest will be destroyed  which of course is not just a problem  uh for the animals and the fauna and  flora that live there  um but of course for global rainfall  patterns and  and indeed climate change as well  richard walker md of iceland thank you  so much for talking to us  thank you
 

